<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with prerendering
       the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit Bsqlite (Bit.Bsqlite) - EF Core on SQLite, inside the browser tab. The same entities and migrations on the client as on the server, over OPFS, IndexedDB, Cache Storage, or memory." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <base href="/" />
    <ResourcePreloader />

    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Crect width='32' height='32' rx='7' fill='%230f766e'/%3E%3Cellipse cx='16' cy='9' rx='9' ry='3.6' fill='none' stroke='%23fff' stroke-width='2.2'/%3E%3Cpath d='M7 9v14c0 2 4 3.6 9 3.6s9-1.6 9-3.6V9' fill='none' stroke='%23fff' stroke-width='2.2'/%3E%3Cpath d='M7 16c0 2 4 3.6 9 3.6s9-1.6 9-3.6' fill='none' stroke='%23fff' stroke-width='2.2'/%3E%3C/svg%3E" />

    @* Runs before first paint so the shell never flashes the wrong theme. The stored value is a
       *preference* ("system" | "light" | "dark"); what lands on <html> is always the resolved one,
       which is why the stylesheet needs a single [data-theme="dark"] block and no duplicated
       prefers-color-scheme table. js/demo.js reads and writes the preference through it. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            var KEY = "bsqlite.theme";
            var media = window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)");

            function preference() {
                try {
                    var stored = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
                    return stored === "light" || stored === "dark" ? stored : "system";
                } catch (e) {
                    return "system"; // storage blocked: the system theme wins
                }
            }

            function apply() {
                var wanted = preference();
                var resolved = wanted === "system" ? (media.matches ? "dark" : "light") : wanted;
                document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", resolved);
                return resolved;
            }

            apply();
            media.addEventListener("change", function () {
                if (preference() === "system") { apply(); }
            });

            window.__bsqliteTheme = { key: KEY, preference: preference, apply: apply };
        })();
    </script>

    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500;600&display=swap" />

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["css/tokens.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["css/base.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["css/components.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["css/landing.css"]" />
    <ImportMap />

    @* Same render mode as the app itself, so <PageTitle> keeps working after hydration. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

<body>
    @* Prerendered on the server, then hydrated by the WebAssembly runtime. Every database this site opens
       lives in the browser - OPFS, IndexedDB, Cache Storage or memory - so none of it can open during the
       prerender pass: each page is readable straight from the served HTML, and its data loads once the
       runtime is interactive (Components/SessionLifecycle.razor). InteractiveWebAssembly rather than Auto or
       Server for the same reason: the worker that owns the database has to run in the user's tab. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />

    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>

    @* Loaded before the Blazor script so window.bsqliteDemo exists by the time the app boots. The library's
       own modules (the worker host, the engine, the VFSes) are imported on demand and need no tag. *@
    <script src="@Assets["js/demo.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
